    <GaugingStation location="Abingdon" name="Ock" number="39018">
        <Comments>
            <Comment type="Station Summary">Peak flow station located just downstream of the current flow measuring structure on the River Ock in Abingdon (39081). It operated between 1962 to 1978.</Comment>
            <Comment type="Hydrometric">Bypassing occurs above 1.3m (12 cumecs), and can be up to 25% of high flows. Consequently, this station is likely to underestimate flows above a 1 in 2 year flood event. One peak rating applied across period of record. Rating by current meter observation.</Comment>
            <Comment type="General">Three adjacent broad crested weirs.</Comment>
            <Comment type="Flow record">Bypassing means station underestimates high flows.</Comment>
            <Comment type="Flow regime">Runoff influenced by groundwater abstraction/recharge. Flows augmented by sewage effluent (derived from outside catchment).</Comment>
            <Comment type="Catchment">Flat, rural valley in the Vale of The White Horse. There is a mixed geology that a high proportion of a pervious nature. Chalk downland forms southern watershed with the remainder mostly Tertiary clays. Characterised by Corallian Beds, Greensand, Chalk, Kimmeridge Clay, Gault &amp; Alluvium. Contributing area exceeds topographical catchment.</Comment>
            <Comment type="Factors affecting runoff">EI</Comment>
        </Comments>
        <Usages>
            <Usage notes="Rating reasonably accurate." suitable="Yes" type="QMED"/>
            <Usage notes="Although the rating is accurate and has plenty of high flow gaugings around AMAX3, the weir is bypassed at 1.3m, which accounts for a significant number of flood events (c.25%)." suitable="No" type="Pooling"/>
        </Usages>
    </GaugingStation>
